LRtriple -- uses Littlewood-Richardson homotopy algorithm to solve a Schubert problem
*************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        LRtriple(n,M)
        LRtriple(n,M, RandomSeed=>12345)
        LRtriple(n,M, WorkingPrecision=>2)
  * Inputs:
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the dimension of the ambient
        space of the Grassmannian $Gr(k,n)$.
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, whose rows encode the
        Schubert condition. Each row is a $k+1$-tuple, $\{m,b\}$, where $m$ is
        a nonegative integer and $b$ a bracket (see bracket2partition for
        details). The bracket $b$ represents a Schubert condition and $m$ is
        its multiplicity in this Schubert problem.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note RandomSeed: LRtriple_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmRandomSeed=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value -1, option to set the seed of the random number
        generators.
      * *note Verbose: PieriRootCount_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbose=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value false, request verbose feedback
      * *note WorkingPrecision: WorkingPrecision, => ..., default value 0,
        option to raise the working precision to double double or quad double.
  * Outputs:
      * a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,, first a string with the
        generic flags, the moved flags, and the solution planes; then a string
        with the polynomial system solved; and thirdly a list of solutions.

Description
===========

LRtriple applies the Littlewood-Richardson homotopies to solve a generic
instance of a Schubert problem defined by at least three Schubert conditions.
The flags defining the instance are generated in the routine.

Use the method parseTriplet to parse the three strings f, p, and s into proper
Macaulay2 polynomials, solution lists, and complex matrices.

The Schubert problem  [2 4 6]^3 on $G(3,6)$ has two solutions. This example
computes the solutions to an instance given by random flags.

