partition2bracket -- dictionary between different notations for Schubert conditions.
************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        b = partition2bracket(l,k,n)
  * Inputs:
      * l, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, partition representing a Schubert
        condition
      * k, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, $k$ and $n$ represent the
        Grassmannian $Gr(k,n)$
  * Outputs:
      * b, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, the corresponding bracket

Description
===========

A Schubert condition in the Grassmannian $Gr(k,n)$ is encoded either by a
partition $l$ or by a bracket $b$.

A partition is a weakly decreasing list of at most $k$ nonnegative integers
less than or equal to $n-k$.  It may be padded with zeroes to be of length $k$.

A bracket is a strictly increasing list of length $k$ of positive integers
between $1$ and $n$.

This function writes a partition as a bracket. They are related as follows
$b_{k+1-i}=n-i-l_i$, for $i=1,...,k$.



+-----------------------------+
|i1 : l = {2,1};              |
+-----------------------------+
|i2 : k = 2;                  |
+-----------------------------+
|i3 : n = 4;                  |
+-----------------------------+
|i4 : partition2bracket(l,k,n)|
|                             |
|o4 = {1, 3}                  |
|                             |
|o4 : List                    |
+-----------------------------+
|i5 : k = 3;                  |
+-----------------------------+
|i6 : n = 6;                  |
+-----------------------------+
|i7 : partition2bracket(l,k,n)|
|                             |
|o7 = {2, 4, 6}               |
|                             |
|o7 : List                    |
+-----------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note bracket2partition: bracket2partition, -- dictionary between different
    notations for Schubert conditions.

