PieriRootCount -- the number of solutions to a generic Pieri problem
********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        r = PieriRootCount(m,p,q)
  * Inputs:
      * m, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the dimension of the input
        planes
      * p, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the dimension of the output
        planes
      * q, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the degree of the solution
        curves producing p-planes
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Verbose: PieriRootCount_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbose=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value false, request verbose feedback
  * Outputs:
      * r, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the number of curves of the
        degree $q$ in the Grassmannian $Gr(p,m+p)$ which produce $p$-planes
        that meet $m\cdot p + q\cdot (m+p)$ generic $m$-planes at given general
        interpolation points. This is a quantum intersection number

Description
===========

The example below computes the number of linear curves which produce 2-planes
that meet 2*3 + 1*(2 + 3) = eleven generic 3-planes at some eleven distinct
interpolation points.

+--------------------------------+
|i1 : r := PieriRootCount(3,2,1);|
+--------------------------------+
|i2 : print r                    |
|55                              |
+--------------------------------+

